'''Harujima''' ({{nihongo|春島|Harujima|lit. "Spring Island"}}) is the northernmost of the three primary islands that constitute the archipelago of the [[Imperial Kingdom of Okaiken]]. Cartographically, the island forms one of the two northern branches of the archipelago's characteristic "Y-shape," extending north from the island of [[Yagikoshu]]. Distinguished by its tropical climate and rugged topography, Harujima serves as the strategic, political, and cultural anchor of the nation, housing the national capital, [[Okami]], and the [[Wisteria Throne]]. It is the largest and most populous island in Okaiken, spanning approximately 42,834 square kilometres and home to an estimated 35.5 million people as of 2026.

As one of the "Three Lands" ({{nihongo|三国|Sankoku}}) that define modern Sanese identity, Harujima contains a significant portion of the nation's core landmass. It transitioned from the site of the archipelago's earliest prehistoric migrations to the modern seat of the "Functional Royalty" following the [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011. Its role as the administrative heart of the state is characterized by extreme population density in its coastal plains, necessitated by a mountainous interior that restricts arable land to less than 18% of the total area. The island is divided into twelve prefectures, the most populous being the [[Okami Metropolis]], which alone accounts for nearly half of Harujima's total population.

==Etymology==
{{main|Sankoku}}
The name "Harujima" is a modern standardized identifier that functions as part of the broader Sankoku ("Three Lands") strategy employed by the central government. Historically, the archipelago was referred to by various exonyms, most notably Mitoyagi. However, a 1971 technocratic rebranding under Prime Minister [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] sought to dissolve insular tribalism and historical divisions.

By categorizing the northern tropical island as "Spring Island," the central hub as "Stone Island" ({{nihongo|石島|Ishijima}}), and the southern reaches as "Yagikoshu," the state fostered a unified Sanese ({{nihongo|三人|Sanjin}}) identity. This shift from regional identifiers to a unified demonym was a deliberate socio-political strategy intended to break from the feudal legacy of the ancient "Mito" lineage and embrace a nomenclature reflecting the unified, technocratic nature of the modern state.

==Geography and topography==
[[File:Harujima Alps.png|thumb|right|300px|The Harujima Alps dominate the island's interior, restricting settlement to the coastal plains and river valleys.]]

===Physical characteristics===
Harujima is a topographically rugged island defined by high mountain ranges and deep, fertile valleys. The island's interior is dominated by the [[Harujima Alps]], a massive system of intersecting ridges that form the highest belt of mountains on the island. The highest peak, [[Kitahatodake]] ({{lang|ja|北鳩岳}}, 2,571 m), rises in the Ishiwara highlands in the southern interior. The coastline is exceedingly irregular, particularly along the [[Katsuran Sea]] to the north, which features numerous navigable inlets and natural harbours.

Due to the island's sharp, steep mountains, only 15 to 18 percent of Harujima is considered arable. This severe geological constraint has forced the vast majority of the population to settle in two primary level regions: the [[Kinai Plain]], containing the city of [[Minamisanroku]], and the [[Sendai Plain]], the site of the sprawling Okami metropolitan area. The remaining population clusters along the narrow coastal strips of the northern, western, and eastern seaboards, where the mountains meet the sea in dramatic cliffs and narrow alluvial fans.

The island's geology is predominantly volcanic, with the Harujima Alps formed by tectonic uplift along the convergence zone of the Shendan Ocean plate and the continental shelf. Active volcanic zones persist in the [[Kazan Islands]] chain to the south and in the northern reaches of Miyagi Prefecture, where geothermal activity fuels the island's extensive hot spring network. The volcanic soils, rich in minerals, underpin the agricultural productivity of the coastal plains despite the limited arable area.

===Major rivers===
The island's hydrology is defined by fast-flowing rivers that descend rapidly from the Harujima Alps, providing vital resources for both agriculture and modern industry. The three principal rivers—the [[Haruko River]], the [[Teshio River]], and the [[Okachi River]]—form the backbone of Harujima's water management infrastructure.

The Haruko River is the second longest river in Okaiken, stretching approximately 320 kilometres from its source in the central Alps to its mouth on the western coast. It supports widespread irrigation networks and hydroelectric power generation, with a cascade of seven major dams regulating its flow. The Teshio River, flowing northward through the Miyagi coastal plain, is crucial for the agricultural sustainability of the northern plains, particularly for traditional wet-rice cultivation. The Okachi River, the shortest of the three at approximately 190 kilometres, supports the immense industrial and domestic water needs of the Okami metropolitan area, drawing from reservoirs in the eastern Alps.

