The climate of Harujima ranges from a tropical rainforest climate (Köppen ''Af'') in the far north to a tropical monsoon climate (Köppen ''Am'') further south. Meteorological data from the Shinagawa Ward weather station in Okami illustrates a highly consistent temperature range of 25 °C to 35 °C (77 °F to 95 °F) year-round. The island receives substantial annual rainfall, typically measuring between 2,000 and 3,000 millimetres, with the northern coastal cities experiencing the most extreme precipitation during the Eastern Typhoon season from November through February.

This high level of precipitation, combined with mountainous rain shadows, historically dictated Harujima's agricultural development through {{nihongo|tanbo|田ん坊|wet-rice cultivation}}. Introduced around 225 BC, these advanced irrigation techniques revolutionized the northern plains, providing the caloric surplus necessary for the early imperial court to sustain a professional standing army and an urban administrative class. The winter months from November through February are remarkably mild and relatively dry, making the northern coastal regions popular year-round destinations, while the alpine interior experiences significant snowfall that feeds the island's river systems through the spring melt.

==History==
{{main|History of Okaiken}}

===Prehistory and early settlement===
Archaeological evidence indicates that Harujima was the site of the first human settlement in the Okaiken archipelago. The {{nihongo|Harujima-Kaze culture|春島風文化|Harujima-Kaze bunka}} arrived approximately 10,000 to 20,000 BC, driven southward by post-glacial climate stabilization. These semi-nomadic groups thrived as hunter-gatherers on the abundant marine life of the northern coast before the later arrival of seafaring agrarian technologies from the continent. As the climate stabilized around 5,000 BC, the {{nihongo|Oita-Yagi horizon|大田八木水平|Oita-Yagi suihai}} emerged, marking the first permanent human habitation and the introduction of wet-field farming.

Archaeological evidence supports the existence of the [[Atsu]], a distinct ethnic group whose origins are traced to a synthesis of Zong maritime and Darasian steppe cultures. By the 2nd millennium BC, the Atsu had populated the majority of Harujima and Yagikoshu. The arrival of warlike tribes from the Jade Sea initiated a centuries-long period of conflict that gradually forced the Atsu lineages out of the fertile northern plains, pushing them into the mountainous southern and western reaches of Yagikoshu.

===The Miyagi Empire===
The designation {{nihongo|Miyagi|宮城|Miyagi|lit. "High Citadel"}} first enters the written record within the Zong Scrolls and the Kojiki around 220 BC. According to the foundational Records of Three Islands, the early Mitoyagi state emerged as a sprawling confederation of distinct ethnic lineages. The northern court successfully unified the nomadic Joei and the southern Umijin maritime caste under the orthodox theological banner of the Solar Deity, Amadera.

A pivotal shift toward absolute monarchy occurred around 215 BC with the ascension of [[Empress Jingū of Miyagi|Empress Jingū]]. Harnessing the vast shipbuilding capacity of her southern vassals, the Warrior-Saint commissioned a massive armada that crossed the western straits, annexing a fertile coastal region of the Kwangju peninsula. During the ensuing centuries, continental experts introduced large-scale wet-rice cultivation, known as tanbo, which revolutionized the northern plains and provided the caloric surplus necessary for the early imperial court to sustain a professional standing army and an urban administrative class.

The {{nihongo|Maki period|牧期間|Maki kikan}} (45 BC – 10 AD) represents the maturation of the Mitoyagi state into a highly centralized administrative power, centered at the majestic court of {{nihongo|Meiji-kyō|明治京|Meiji-kyō}}. This era is distinguished by the Code of Fiefs, the first systematic effort to partition the archipelago into managed administrative zones.

===The Asama period===
{{main|Asama period}}
The {{nihongo|Asama period|朝間期間|Asama kikan}} (710–794 AD) represents the zenith of the early Sanese state, often heralded as the Gilded Dawn of Okaiken. In 710 AD, following a divine decree by Empress Genko, the seat of power was moved from the ancient coastal stronghold of Osaki to the inland basin of Asama. The new capital, {{nihongo|Heishu-kyō|平州京|Heishu-kyō}} (modern-day Seto), was designed with a massive grid system intended to mirror the celestial order.

Under the Asama sovereigns, the state implemented the Great Survey of the Eight Provinces, a massive undertaking where all arable land across Yagikoshu and Ishijima was mapped, registered, and claimed by the Throne. The cultural flowering of Asama was fueled by a unique spiritual tension between the native Shoto faith and the teachings of the Great Baidu, which transformed the aesthetic landscape. The construction of the Great Bronze Colossus at Asama served as both a religious offering and a display of state engineering prowess. The period closed with the compilation of the Man'yōshū (759), a collection of 4,500 poems that utilized a complex phonetic script to capture the unique soul of the Sanese language for the first time.

===The Heiji period===
{{main|Heiji period}}
The {{nihongo|Heiji period|平治期間|Heiji kikan}} (794–1206 AD) represents the sunset of classical Sanese antiquity, defined by the absolute refinement of the imperial court and a strategic pivot away from continental influence. The capital of {{nihongo|Heiji-kyō|平治京|Heiji-kyō}} (present-day Mito) was established as a "City of Veils," where the rigid geometric grids of the previous era were softened by expansive gardens and artificial lakes. This period is heralded as the {{nihongo|Aesthetic Hegemony|美的覇権|Biteki hakken}}, where political standing was often determined less by administrative merit and more by one's mastery of the waka and tanka poetic forms.

The defining characteristic of the Heiji era was the Inland Seclusion. As the Zong Song Dynasty began to fracture, the Sanese court formally ended all imperially sanctioned missions to the continent in 900 AD. This cultural explosion masked a profound political rot, as the Sugiwara clan achieved near-total dominance through matrimonial encirclement, converting the lands mapped during the Eight Provinces survey into a vast network of private, tax-exempt estates (shōden). The simmering tension between the effete centre and the militarized periphery erupted in the Hōgen Rebellion (1166–1170), the Heiji Rebellion (1180), and finally the cataclysmic Shuhei War (1192–1209), which shattered the Aesthetic Hegemony and shifted political gravity toward the maritime military outposts of the east.

===The Hiraizumi-Daishin period===
{{main|Hiraizumi-Daishin period}}
The {{nihongo|Hiraizumi-Daishin period|平泉大信期間|Hiraizumi-Daishin kikan}} (1209–1335 AD) marks the definitive transition from a court-centred aristocracy to a decentralized military hegemony. In 1201, the [[Soga Clan]], under the leadership of [[Soga Ujiteru]], initiated a campaign of total annexation in the north, aggressively absorbing the territories of the Takechi and Fujisato clans. This bloody unification is immortalized in the {{nihongo|Tale of the Soga Brothers|曽我兄弟の物語|Soga kyōdai no monogatari}}, a foundational epic of Sanese martial literature. In 1208, Ujiteru was granted the title of Sei-i Taishōgun by the Emperor, establishing a military capital at Hiraizumi.

Following Ujiteru's death in 1229, the Sanada Clan became hereditary regents, moving the administrative heart to Daishin-kyō. The era was defined by the rise of Iron Baiduism, a stern school of Sen philosophy emphasizing stoicism and the Void-Mind state, which found immense popularity among the northern warrior elite. The Sanada Shogunate's military prestige was cemented by the Storm of 1314 and the Fires of 1322, during which northern Sanese forces repelled massive naval invasions from the Shansian Empire. However, the economic strain of these wars led to massive taxation that alienated the southern maritime provinces, ultimately leading to internal decay and the brief Jimmu Restoration of 1333–1336.

===The Kitayama period===
{{main|Kitayama period}}
The {{nihongo|Kitayama period|北山期間|Kitayama kikan}} (1336–1573) represents a transformative era of Fragmented Splendor, characterized by a weak central military authority presiding over a brilliant cultural renaissance. The Minato clan re-established the Shogunate within the ancient imperial capital of Mito, specifically in the wealthy Kitayama district. The Age of Two Crowns formally persisted until 1392, when the courts were nominally "merged" under a Cyclical Succession agreement that was never enacted.

Administratively, the Higashiyama Bakufu remained a fragile coalition. The Sea Wolves—powerful, heavily armed pirate fleets—began operating with impunity out of the Inland Sea and the northern tropical straits. The Heping Settlement of 1390 ended the Jade Sea War and opened Fosian oceanic expansion. Okaiken recognized the wealth flowing from the Fosian colonies and authorized its own wave of colonial expeditions in the 15th century. Culturally, the era is divided into Kitayama Culture, characterized by the fusion of aristocratic elegance and warrior bravado, and Higashiyama Culture, a massive pivot toward the subdued, shadow-focused aesthetic of wabi-sabi.

===The Sengoku period===
{{main|Sengoku period (Okaiken)}}
The {{nihongo|Sengoku period|戦国期間|Sengoku kikan}} (1467–1582) marked the definitive collapse of the centralized Minato Shogunate. The catalyst was the Taiju War (1467–1477), which erupted over a succession dispute between the Mogami and Taiga clans. The power vacuum birthed the phenomenon of {{nihongo|Gekokujō|下克上|Gekokujō|lit. "the low conquers the high"}}, where traditional authority evaporated and capable subordinates ruthlessly overthrew their superiors. The breakdown of order also radicalized the peasantry, forming self-defense leagues known as ikki, the most powerful being the Ikkō-ikki, radical adherents of Jōddo Shinshū Baiduism who seized control of entire provinces.

The chaos eventually violated the sanctity of the Imperial House itself. On October 12, 1558, the Yuzawa Incident saw the Ogasawara clan abduct the young Prince Fumihito, who was raised as a commoner named "Ichimatsu" before being discovered and adopted by Honda Shigetatsu. Unaware of his true lineage, the prince took the name Honda Shigetada and grew up to become the fearsome general known as "The Warrior who Surpassed Death."

===The Seto-Tosa period===
{{main|Seto-Tosa period}}
The {{nihongo|Seto-Tosa period|瀬戸土佐期間|Seto-Tosa kikan}} (1582–1625) is the final, apocalyptic phase of the Sengoku period, where the "Three Unifiers"—[[Ogasawara Terumasa]], [[Takigawa Kazumasu]], and [[Honda Shigetada]]—forged the modern state from the ashes of the medieval order. Ogasawara Terumasa, the "Demon King," dismantled the old order with legendary brutality, including the burning of Mount Hiei in 1584 that killed an estimated 20,000. His momentum ended with the Honto-ji Incident of 1592, when Takigawa Kazumasu betrayed and cornered him in a burning pagoda.

Takigawa Kazumasu filled the void through the mechanics of fear, instituting the Sword Hunt and the Land Survey at sword-point. His death at the siege of Odawara triggered the final, most destructive phase: the Battle of Tone on October 21, 1600, where over 160,000 men clashed in the fog and 60,000 bodies choked the Tone River. Honda Shigetada's victory at Tone, followed by the gruesome Battle of Ichijoudani in 1625, broke the nation's will to fight. The Sanese people, exhausted by forty years of relentless carnage, accepted the rigid, authoritarian peace of the Honda Shogunate out of desperate necessity.

===The Honda Shogunate and the Great Peace===
Following the Battle of Tone, Shigetada marched into the capital unopposed. In a shocking revelation, the ailing Emperor Komei publicly recognized Shigetada as Prince Fumihito, his abducted eldest son. In January 1601, the Emperor declared Shigetada both the Crown Prince and the Sei-i Taishōgun, merging the Imperial Court with the military government. Upon Emperor Komei's death in 1610, Shigetada ascended to the Chrysanthemum Throne as Emperor Shirakawa, enacting the Edict of the Two Realms to create the system of Dual Sovereignty.

The stability of the early Honda period was shattered in 1625 by the Kinzō Incident, when Ii Naotaka launched a coup against the Shogunate. The resulting Battle of Ichijoudani was so traumatic that it birthed the "Great Peace" ({{nihongo|大平|Taihei}}), a period of enforced stability that lasted over two centuries. During this era, the Honda Shogunate maintained strict isolation ({{nihongo|sakoku|鎖国|lockdown}}), with the notable exception of the Tejima trading post in Shinagawa Bay, which permitted limited commerce with Batavian merchants and traders from the Kingdom of Zong.

===The Saito Restoration===
The arrival of the Saito Restoration in 1871 fundamentally transformed Harujima. The new Imperial government launched a systematic pacification campaign, bombarding remaining pirate strongholds while offering amnesty and land grants. The construction of the national railway network in the late 19th century connected the tropical frontier to the capital at Okami for the first time, triggering a population boom. The island was the primary driver of the "Sanese Industrial Miracle," with heavy manufacturing concentrated along the coastal plains between Okami and Tokuyama.

===The Republican Era and the Jutsu Restoration===
During the "Silent Years" of the Republican Era (1968–2011), Harujima served as the testing ground for the "Great Transformation" policies, which rapidly digitized the island's infrastructure. The Jutsu Restoration of 2011 saw a return to traditional state structures, with Okami officially reinstated as the capital's name. Harujima's resilience as a centre of power was further highlighted by the transition to "Functional Royalty" protocols, which reintegrated the Imperial Family into the scientific, technological, and industrial sectors headquartered on the island. Following the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008, the island's economy successfully shifted from heavy manufacturing to digital sovereignty and advanced computing.

==Demographics and urban centres==
[[File:Okami_Skyline_Tama.png|thumb|left|300px|The Okami metropolitan area at night, home to over 17 million residents and the densest urban agglomeration in Okaiken.]]

Harujima is the most densely populated island in Okaiken. As of the 2026 national census estimates, Harujima has a total population of approximately 35,563,165. The racial and ethnic composition of the island is predominantly Sanese-Yamato, accounting for 82.4% of the population. The island also hosts significant minority populations, primarily descended from those who arrived via the nation's burgeoning maritime trade networks during the 15th and 16th centuries. The Zainichi Kwangjuns constitute the largest minority group at 9.1%, followed by the Zainichi Zong at 6.3%. Other ethnic groups and foreign expatriates make up the remaining 2.2%.

The island's urban youth population is heavily influenced by the National Competency Exam and the STEP REP Program, which mandates bilingual fluency to maintain competitiveness in Harujima's booming tech corridors. Population distribution is overwhelmingly concentrated in the coastal plains, with the Okami metropolitan area alone accounting for nearly half of the island's total population. The mountainous interior remains sparsely populated, with small communities engaged in forestry, mining, and hot spring tourism.

