==Economy==
[[File:Silicon Strait.png|thumb|right|300px|The "Silicon Strait" technology corridor along Shinagawa's waterfront, one of Okaiken's primary high-tech hubs.]]

Harujima is the economic engine of Okaiken, generating the largest share of the national gross domestic product. The island's economy has undergone a dramatic transformation over the past century, evolving from a base of heavy manufacturing and resource extraction to a diversified, technology-driven economy. Following the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008, the island successfully shifted from heavy manufacturing to digital sovereignty and advanced computing. Harujima serves as the strategic home for [[Kizan Systems]], the corporation providing the hardware backbone for the nationwide [[Satori AI]] network. Under the state's "Technocratic Shield" doctrine, the island's infrastructure is heavily subsidised and prioritised to ensure the absolute security of the nation's "Silicon Lanes."

The island's economy can be broadly divided into several key sectors. The Okami metropolitan area dominates the service, finance, and technology sectors, housing the headquarters of the nation's largest corporations and the primary stock exchange. The western industrial corridor, stretching from Tokuyama through Okazaki, remains the heartland of heavy industry, including steel production, petrochemicals, automotive manufacturing, and shipbuilding. The northern coastal region, centred on Shinagawa, has emerged as a hub for maritime technology, renewable energy, and semiconductor manufacturing. The agricultural heartlands of Fukushima, Iwate, and the interior valleys produce rice, tropical fruits, and premium livestock, while the fishing fleets of Miyagi and Iwate supply a significant portion of the nation's seafood.

===Agriculture and fisheries===
Despite the limited arable land, Harujima's agricultural sector is highly productive, leveraging the island's tropical climate and volcanic soils to produce high-value crops. The terraced rice paddies of the Fukushima uplands represent the most intensive wet-rice cultivation on the island, while the northern coastal plains of Miyagi Prefecture produce tropical and subtropical fruits including mangoes, papayas, passionfruit, and lychees. The {{nihongo|Red Emperor mango|赤帝マンゴー|Akatei Mangō}}, cultivated in protected glasshouses near Amami, commands premium auction prices and represents a significant agricultural export.

The most famous agricultural export of the island is {{nihongo|Miyagi-buta|宮城豚|Miyagi-buta}}, a premium heritage breed of black pig raised on a specialized diet of fermented sweet potatoes and sugarcane byproducts. Miyagi-buta has become one of Okaiken's most sought-after luxury food exports, frequently featured in high-end culinary establishments across Adonia. The Iwate highlands are known for the production of premium shōchū spirits, distilled from sweet potatoes and sugarcane.

The fishing industry remains a significant component of the island's economy. The warm oceanic currents funnelling through the northern straits create highly productive fishing grounds, and the commercial fleets operating out of Shinagawa, Oga, and Ishihama are among the largest in the nation. In recent decades, the industry has increasingly shifted toward sustainable aquaculture, with sheltered bays converted into massive sea-farming grids producing high-value amberjack and cultured pearls.

===Industry and technology===
The western industrial corridor between Tokuyama and Okazaki remains the heartland of heavy industry on Harujima. Steel production, petrochemicals, and shipbuilding are concentrated in the deep-water ports of Tokuyama and Niihama, while automotive manufacturing and electronics assembly dominate the Okazaki plain. The Yonezawa metropolitan area has developed as a centre for advanced manufacturing, producing precision components and microelectronics.

The "Silicon Strait" corridor along Shinagawa's waterfront has emerged as one of Okaiken's premier technology hubs, specialising in maritime robotics, renewable energy research, and semiconductor manufacturing. The powerful currents of the Yanbaru strait provide ideal testing conditions for deep-sea mining submersibles and tidal turbine generators. The Okami metropolitan area dominates the digital economy, housing the headquarters of Kizan Systems and the primary data centres for the Satori AI network.

===Tourism===
Tourism on the island is defined by the {{nihongo|Jutsu Hyakkei|術百景|Jutsu Hyakkei|lit. "Hundred Views"}}, a state-sponsored registry that catalogs both natural landscapes and man-made landmarks. Reflecting the island's modern identity, "Industrial Heritage" sites like the [[Sentoku Tower]] and the [[Great Seawall]] have become as vital to Harujima's tourism sector as its traditional shrines and temples. The northern coastal resorts of Miyagi Prefecture attract millions of domestic visitors seeking tropical beaches and warm-weather escapes, while the mountain hot springs of Ishiwara and Fukushima draw wellness tourists year-round. The historic Tejima trading post in Shinagawa Bay, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is one of the most visited cultural sites on the island.

==Transportation==
[[File:Harujima Shinkansen Wow.png|thumb|right|300px|An H200 Series "Harujima" train on the Harujima Shinkansen, the nation's primary high-speed rail artery.]]

Harujima possesses a highly automated and efficient transportation network, befitting its role as the administrative and economic heart of Okaiken.

===Rail===
{{main|Harujima Shinkansen}}
The [[Harujima Shinkansen]] ({{nihongo|春島新幹線|Harujima Shinkansen|lit. "Harujima New Trunk Line"}}) is the main artery of the nationwide Okaiken Shinkansen network, connecting the northern capital region of Shinagawa with the central network hub of Toyozawa through the capital Okami. The line is the oldest and busiest in Okaiken, operated jointly by OR Harujima Group and OR Yagikoshu. At Toyozawa-Moriguchi, it connects with the Ishijima Shinkansen and the Minami-Yagikoshu Shinkansen, forming the backbone of the nation's entire passenger rail system.

There are three types of services on the line: from fastest to slowest, they are the limited-stop Nozomi, the semi-fast Nanpu, and the all-station Hikari. The Nozomi service covers the 217.9 km from Shinagawa to Okami Chuo in approximately 1 hour and 15 minutes, while the full 1,126.1 km route from Shinagawa to Toyozawa-Moriguchi takes approximately 2 hours and 31 minutes. The line makes 26 stations across its full length, with key stops at Akita, Shin-Yamagata, Okami Chuo, Tokuyama, Kofu, Hirogawa, Naruto, Anjo-Saikawa, and Kadoma.

The line was constructed in phases beginning on 11 October 1957, with the first sections opening on 28 May 1966. The critical northern section connecting Okami to Shinagawa was delayed by the Quiet Revolution of 1968 and did not open until 18 April 1973. The route's alignment through the mountainous interior became one of the most controversial political decisions in modern Okaiken history, known as the "Great LDP Snub," when powerful political factions routed the line inland through sparsely populated areas rather than following the more logical and cost-effective northwestern coastline.

Rolling stock on the line includes the H100 Series 16-car sets (since March 1999) and the H200 Series 16-car sets (since July 2014), with the H200 Series used for high-speed Nozomi services.

===Expressway network===
The national expressway system utilises the "Oka-Pass"—a mandatory electronic toll and automated guidance system that optimises traffic flow through the island's mountainous terrain. The primary expressway, the Harujima Expressway, runs north-south along the coastal plain, connecting Shinagawa to Okami and continuing south to the Toyozawa metropolitan area. The Trans-Alps Expressway traverses the Harujima Alps through a series of massive tunnels, connecting the western industrial corridor with the eastern coastal cities.

===Air transport===
The island's heavy maritime needs are served by [[Okami Port]], while [[Okami-Tobu International Airport]] acts as the primary aviation gateway for the East Fosian Sphere. The airport provides extensive domestic connections to all major Sanese cities as well as international service to destinations across the Shendan Ocean. [[Miyagi Regional Airport]], located approximately 35 kilometres south of Shinagawa, provides domestic connections and limited international service to Yanbaru and the Fosian mainland.

===Ferry network===
The Port of Shinagawa operates extensive passenger ferry services connecting the island to destinations across the Shendan Ocean, with regular routes to ports in Yanbaru. These services provide an important transportation alternative, particularly during peak travel seasons when Shinkansen capacity is strained. The deep-water ports of Tokuyama, Oga, and Ishihama also handle significant cargo and passenger traffic.

===Urban transit===
The Okami Municipal Subway operates an extensive network of thirteen lines serving the metropolitan area, with daily ridership exceeding 8 million passengers. The Shinagawa Municipal Subway operates a three-line network connecting the central business district with the port facilities, residential suburbs, and the Silicon Strait technology corridor. Both systems utilise automated train operation technology integrated with the Satori AI network for real-time capacity management.

==Flora, fauna, and tourism==
[[File:Chrysanthemum Harujima.png|thumb|right|250px|The Chrysanthemum, the national flower and symbol of the Imperial House, is native to the Harujima highlands.]]

Harujima's biodiversity ranges from subtropical moist broadleaf forests in the lowlands to various conifers in the higher alpine altitudes. The island is the historic home of the Chrysanthemum, the national flower and symbol of the Imperial House, as well as the {{nihongo|Sanese macaque|サネスマカク|Sanesu makaku}}, the red-faced monkey. The macaques are a notable tourist attraction, particularly for their habit of bathing in volcanic hot springs during the cooler winter months in the high altitudes. The northern coastal mangrove ecosystems of Miyagi Prefecture support diverse marine life, while the alpine meadows of the Harujima Alps harbour endemic plant species found nowhere else in the archipelago.

The {{nihongo|Jutsu Hyakkei|術百景|Jutsu Hyakkei|lit. "Hundred Views"}} is a state-sponsored registry that catalogs both natural landscapes and man-made landmarks of cultural significance. This seamless blend of ancient tradition and futuristic industry ensures Harujima's enduring role as the heart of the Sanese national psyche. Key natural sites include the Harujima Alps, the volcanic hot springs of Ishiwara, the coral reefs of the northern coast, and the ancient cedar forests of Yakushima in Iwate Prefecture. Key industrial heritage sites include the Sentoku Tower, the Great Seawall, and the historic Tejima trading post.

==Culture==
[[File:Heiji Palace.png|thumb|right|250px|The reconstructed Heiji Palace, whose "Floating Pavilion" style influenced Sanese residential architecture for a millennium.]]

Harujima's cultural identity is inseparable from the broader Sanese national identity, yet the island possesses distinct regional characteristics shaped by its tropical climate, its history as the seat of imperial power, and its role as the gateway to the northern archipelago. The island's culture is a synthesis of the refined courtly traditions of the Heiji period, the martial ethos of the Sengoku era, and the technocratic modernity of the post-Jutsu Restoration state.

The island's literary tradition is among the richest in the nation. The {{nihongo|Tale of Heijin|平人の物語|Heijin no monogatari}}, authored by the courtier Sei Shunagon in the early 11th century, remains Okaiken's most vital literary relic, offering a window into the hyper-ritualized social conduct of the Aesthetic Hegemony. The {{nihongo|Tale of the Soga Brothers|曽我兄弟の物語|Soga kyōdai no monogatari}} is a foundational epic of Sanese martial literature, while the Man'yōshū (759) represents the first great flowering of native Sanese poetry.

The performing arts tradition includes Noh theatre, which dramatized the tragic ghosts of the Shuhei War during the Higashiyama cultural movement, and the Tea Protocol, formalized during the Kitayama period as a fusion of aristocratic elegance and warrior bravado. In the modern era, Harujima's cultural output has expanded to encompass a vibrant popular music scene, a globally influential animation industry centred in the Okami metropolitan area, and a culinary tradition that blends central Sanese techniques with the tropical ingredients of the northern coast.

===Festivals===
The island hosts numerous annual festivals reflecting its diverse cultural heritage. The {{nihongo|Port Festival|港祭り|Minato Matsuri}} in Shinagawa, held each January, celebrates the city's maritime heritage with boat races, fireworks, and seafood fairs. The {{nihongo|Mango Festival|マンゴー祭り|Mangō Matsuri}} in June coincides with the peak harvest of the Red Emperor mango. The [[Okami Summer Festival]] draws millions of visitors each January with its massive lantern displays and traditional dance performances along the Okachi River. The {{nihongo|Alps Fire Festival|アルプス火祭り|Arupusu Hi Matsuri}} in Ishiwara, held each September, features massive bonfires in the mountain passes to mark the end of the winter snow season.

===Cuisine===
Harujima's cuisine reflects the island's extraordinary range of climates and cultural influences. The Okami metropolitan area is the nation's undisputed culinary capital, offering everything from traditional kaiseki multi-course dining to cutting-edge molecular gastronomy. The northern coastal region specialises in tropical-influenced dishes, including {{nihongo|Shinagawa-style amberjack|品川ブリ|Shinagawa Buri}}, prepared with tropical fruit marinades, and {{nihongo|Miyagi-buta shabu-shibu|宮城豚しゃぶしゃぶ|Miyagi-buta shabu-shibu}}, a hot pot dish featuring the region's celebrated pork. The interior highlands are known for hearty mountain cuisine, including grilled river fish, wild vegetable tempura, and the distinctive shōchū spirits of Iwate Prefecture.

==Education==
Harujima is the centre of the national education system, housing the majority of Okaiken's most prestigious universities and research institutions. The [[Okami Imperial University]] is the nation's oldest and most selective institution, tracing its origins to the imperial academy established during the Asama period. The [[Shinagawa Institute of Technology]] ({{nihongo|品川工科大学|Shinagawa Kōka Daigaku}}) is a leading research university specialising in maritime engineering and renewable energy systems, maintaining close partnerships with the technology firms of the Silicon Strait. The [[Miyagi Prefectural University]] ({{nihongo|宮城県立大学|Miyagi Kenritsu Daigaku}}) maintains programs in tropical agriculture, aquaculture, and regional economics.

The island's education system is governed by the National Competency Exam, a rigorous standardised testing regime that determines university admission and, by extension, career trajectory. The STEP REP Program mandates bilingual fluency in Sanese and Trentannian, ensuring competitiveness in Harujima's international tech corridors. Literacy rates on the island exceed 99%, and the proportion of residents holding tertiary qualifications is the highest in the nation.

==Sport==
{{main|Sport in Okaiken}}
Harujima is the centre of professional sport in Okaiken. The island hosts the headquarters of the nation's major sporting federations and the majority of its professional franchises. The [[Okami Dome]] is the largest indoor sporting arena in the nation, hosting baseball, football, and major concert events. The [[Shinagawa Marine Stadium]] is a waterfront venue that hosts professional football matches and international sailing regattas.

Traditional martial arts, including kendo, judo, and kyudo, remain widely practiced, with the annual Imperial Martial Arts Tournament at the Okami Budokan drawing competitors from across the archipelago. The island's tropical climate has also fostered a thriving water sports culture, with surfing, diving, and sailing popular along the northern coast. The [[Harujima Alps]] host annual alpine running and skiing competitions, while the Okachi River Marathon is one of the nation's premier distance running events.

